ay to people no ice no life it's  essential for life on earth because ice  keeps our planet within a temperature  range in which we can live and so now as  the arctic is beginning to melt and down  in the southern hemisphere as antarctica  is also uh melting and also the glaciers  on the high mountains of the world  this has a huge impact on us all over  the world so what's happening now in a  lula set  will literally impact every single  person on this planet every future  generation and the whole of the animal  kingdom  when it comes to the glaciers on the  mountains  these glaciers provide a constant water  supply  to villages towns cities all the way  downstream so obviously  ice is essential  it's as important as the air we breathe  okay and unless we get a grip on this  climate crisis unless we're able to turn  this ship around and get all the world  leaders now who are meeting at the end  of the year in glasgow  to take action we've had 27 years now of  negotiations  think about that 27 years of  negotiations  um unless we're able to get them to make  firm commitments and take action today  that  if if we can't get that  then our our future is  is is is very bleak  louis in your mind then given that which  you've seen well at the the ice fjord  and on all your trips around the world  is the damage that has been done to the  planet reversible or is the campaign as  much as anything else about slowing the  rate of damage that we're doing to our  world  i mean that's certainly a question which  a climate scientist would would be able  to answer i'm a maritime lawyer with  respect but certainly from what i what  i've been seeing now the speed of this  uh melt  every single year i'm seeing it getting  faster and faster when i did my first  swim in the arctic  in 2003 the sea ice was three degree i  mean the the the water temperature was  three degrees centigrade  i went back there 12 years later and the  water was in the arctic was no longer  three degrees centigr